// What stands between two operands is written `[x]`, and a rule may ask for
// two of them around a shared operand. The rule says which way they group, and
// its body applies what it was handed: `[x]` is not a name for the operator,
// it is the operator, written where the body writes it.

mark ^keyword := external global

// The language chains comparisons of its own: the entrypoint writes the rule
// and `chainable` says which operators it takes. Written through, a comparison
// says both of the comparisons it is written as, so a chain that breaks
// anywhere is false.
unless (one < two < three) { INFO@mark `PR3 one below two below three does not hold` }
if (three < two < one) { INFO@mark `PR4 three below two below one holds` }
if (one < three < two) { INFO@mark `PR5 a chain that breaks at the end holds` }
if (two < one < three) { INFO@mark `PR6 a chain that breaks at the start holds` }
unless (three > two > one) { INFO@mark `PR16 three above two above one does not hold` }
if (one > two > three) { INFO@mark `PR17 one above two above three holds` }
unless (one <= two <= three) { INFO@mark `PR18 a chain of at-mosts does not hold` }
if (one <= three <= two) { INFO@mark `PR19 a chain of at-mosts that breaks holds` }
unless (three >= two >= one) { INFO@mark `PR20 a chain of at-leasts does not hold` }
unless (one == one == one) { INFO@mark `PR21 a chain of sames does not hold` }
if (one == one == two) { INFO@mark `PR22 a chain of sames that breaks holds` }

// And one written here, to show that a rule of this shape does what it says.
// Which operators it will take is answered by a class, the way the type of a
// capture is; it names its own captures, since a rule is told from another by
// what it is called and an operator's filter is part of that.
minus ^keyword := class {
  accepts (written) => if (exists (external alike (written) (`-`))) { written } else { None }
}
{start} [takes: minus] {once} [again: minus] {twice} => (start [takes] once) [again] twice
unless ((ten - two - three) == five) { INFO@mark `PR1 ten less two less three is not five` }
unless (((ten + ten) - four - six) == ten) { INFO@mark `PR2 twenty less four less six is not ten` }

// What is written with one of them is untouched, and so is what the filter
// does not take.
unless ((one + two) == three) { INFO@mark `PR7 one and two is not three` }
unless (one < two) { INFO@mark `PR8 one is not below two` }
if (two < one) { INFO@mark `PR9 two is below one` }
unless ((two * three) == six) { INFO@mark `PR10 an operator the rule does not ask for stopped working` }
unless ((ten - two) == eight) { INFO@mark `PR11 one less on its own stopped working` }
unless ((one + two + three) == six) { INFO@mark `PR12 three added is not six` }

// Two operators of different kinds group by where they are written: of `+`,
// `-` and `*`, written in that order in Number, the later binds tighter, and
// one written as often as the other groups to the left.
unless ((two * three + one) == seven) { INFO@mark `PR23 two times three and one is not seven` }
unless ((one + two * three) == seven) { INFO@mark `PR24 one and two times three is not seven` }
unless ((ten / two / five) == one) { INFO@mark `PR25 ten shared twice is not one` }
unless ((two * three * two) == (ten + two)) { INFO@mark `PR26 two times three times two is not twelve` }
unless ((ten - four * two) == two) { INFO@mark `PR27 ten less four times two is not two` }
unless (one + two < four) { INFO@mark `PR28 one and two is not below four` }
if (four < one + two) { INFO@mark `PR29 four is below one and two` }
unless (one + two == three) { INFO@mark `PR30 one and two is not three` }
unless (three == one + two) { INFO@mark `PR31 three is not one and two` }
if (two * three < five) { INFO@mark `PR32 two times three is below five` }

// spec: a chain of three comparisons is not claimed — the rule takes two
// operators around a shared operand, so `a < b < c < d` reads as
// `(a < b and b < c) < d`, which compares a boolean with a number. The draft
// reduces a whole run at once (.ray2/Grammar.ray:175-205); this reduces a pair.
//
// spec: a chain written with an operator of two characters is read right and
// reported wrong: four errors per chain, all `Unexpected =`. Nothing in the
// library writes one, so it costs nothing there.
